1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,825
How Does Wi-Fi Actually Work?

2
00:00:02,775 --> 00:00:19,050
Wi-Fi transmits data using radio waves in the unlicensed ISM (industrial, scientific, and medical) frequency bands, primarily 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz, with the 6 GHz band opened for Wi-Fi 6E and Wi-Fi 7.

3
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:29,875
Data transmission employs orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM), which divides a single data stream across dozens or hundreds of closely spaced subcarrier frequencies.

4
00:00:29,875 --> 00:00:37,512
This approach provides high spectral efficiency while resisting interference and multipath fading caused by signal reflections.

5
00:00:37,512 --> 00:00:45,362
The Medium Access Control (MAC) layer uses carrier-sense multiple access with collision avoidance (CSMA/CA).

6
00:00:45,362 --> 00:00:55,637
Before transmitting, a device listens for ongoing traffic; if the channel is clear, it sends data; if busy, it waits a random backoff period before retrying.

7
00:00:55,637 --> 00:01:12,175
Multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) technology, introduced with 802.11n (Wi-Fi 4) in 2009, uses multiple antennas to transmit and receive several data streams simultaneously, multiplying throughput linearly with antenna count.

8
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:37,150
The IEEE 802.11 family has evolved substantially since the original 1997 standard operating at 2 Mbps. Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax, 2019) introduces orthogonal frequency-division multiple access (OFDMA), which subdivides channels efficiently among many devices simultaneously — critical for dense environments such as stadiums and office buildings.

9
00:01:37,150 --> 00:01:49,550
Wi-Fi 7 (802.11be) promises maximum data rates exceeding 30 Gbps through 320 MHz channel width and 4096-QAM modulation.

10
00:01:49,550 --> 00:02:02,675
The branding name Wi-Fi, chosen by the branding firm Interbrand in 1999, does not stand for anything — it was selected for recognizability, contrary to the persistent myth that it abbreviates "wireless fidelity.

11
00:02:02,675 --> 00:02:13,537
Signal attenuation follows the inverse square law, but obstacles add significant losses: a typical concrete wall attenuates 2.4 GHz signals by 8-15 dB.

12
00:02:13,537 --> 00:02:25,275
Mesh networking systems address coverage gaps by deploying multiple access points that communicate on a dedicated wireless backhaul channel, handing off client devices seamlessly as they move throughout a building.

